### ============================================================
### Google Geocoding — an address to a coordinate, and back (#357)
###
### The key lives in `http/http-client.private.env.json`, beside the public env file
### and gitignored. Copy the template and fill in one environment:
###
###     cp http/http-client.private.env.json.example http/http-client.private.env.json
###
### **The IDE finds that copy on its own. ijhttp does not** — without the flag nothing
### defines the variable, the request goes out carrying a literal
### `key={{google-maps-api-key}}`, and Google refuses it with REQUEST_DENIED:
###
###     cd http && ijhttp --env-file http-client.env.json \
###         --private-env-file http-client.private.env.json --env local google/geocoding.http
###
### `scripts/geocode-venues.py` reads the same file, so the key is stored once.
### The script is the better tool for all 86 venues at once — it caches, compares
### against what we store, and ranks by distance. This file is for the single
### lookup while adding a venue, and for reading a full response.
###
### Deliberately not in `./gradlew httpTest`. Every other .http file there talks to
### a local importer; these leave the machine, are billed, and need a key CI has no
### reason to hold.
###
### **Two syntax traps, both silent.** A comma in an `@variable` value makes ijhttp
### skip the request and still report `RUN SUCCESSFUL` with 0 requests completed, so
### the address goes in the request line, where spaces, umlauts and `ß` are all safe
### — the client encodes the URL it builds. And a request is named after the *first*
### `###` line above it, not the last, so each block below opens with its title and
### explains itself underneath.
###
### **Do not paste a coordinate straight into the database without reading
### `scripts/geocode-venues.py`'s module docstring.** Storing a Google latitude and
### longitude for longer than 30 days, on a map that is not Google's, is outside the
### Maps Platform terms. Everything in the `venue` table today is OpenStreetMap's,
### ODbL, attributed where it is displayed.
### ============================================================

### Geocode one venue address
### Replace the address and run. `components` names the country as a restriction and
### Berlin only as a hint: adding `postal_code:10245` would force the answer into the
### postal code we already believe, and the disagreement between ours and Google's is
### the signal that an *address* is wrong rather than a coordinate. #329 closed with
### `Mikropol` unresolved for exactly that reason, so the filter stays loose.
GET https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/geocode/json?address=Revaler Str. 99, 10245 Berlin&components=country:DE|locality:Berlin&language=de&region=de&key={{google-maps-api-key}}
Accept: application/json

> {%
    client.test("status is 200", function () {
        client.assert(response.status === 200, "Expected 200 but got " + response.status);
    });
    client.test("Google found the address", function () {
        client.assert(response.body.status === "OK",
            response.body.status + " " + (response.body.error_message || ""));
    });

    // location_type is the field that decides whether to believe the pair. ROOFTOP is the
    // building; APPROXIMATE is Google shrugging at the district, and the distance from what
    // we store then measures its vagueness rather than our error.
    if (response.body.status === "OK") {
        const top = response.body.results[0];
        client.log(top.geometry.location.lat.toFixed(6) + ", " + top.geometry.location.lng.toFixed(6));
        client.log(top.geometry.location_type + (top.partial_match ? " (partial match)" : ""));
        client.log(top.formatted_address);
        client.log(response.body.results.length + " result(s)");
    }
%}

### Reverse — what is actually at a coordinate we already store?
### The other direction of the same question, and the quicker one when a pin looks
### wrong on a map: hand it the stored pair and read back the address it points at.
GET https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/geocode/json?latlng=52.507242,13.451803&language=de&result_type=street_address&key={{google-maps-api-key}}
Accept: application/json

> {%
    client.test("status is 200", function () {
        client.assert(response.status === 200, "Expected 200 but got " + response.status);
    });
    client.test("the point resolves to an address", function () {
        client.assert(response.body.status === "OK",
            response.body.status + " " + (response.body.error_message || ""));
    });
    if (response.body.status === "OK") {
        client.log(response.body.results[0].formatted_address);
    }
%}

### Setup check — proves the key, the quota and the request shape
### Astra Kulturhaus, Revaler Str. 99, a venue we hold. If this fails, the failure is
### the setup rather than the venue. The bound is deliberately loose: it asserts
### Berlin, not a doorway — the same bound `VenueRequest` carries.
GET https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/geocode/json?address=Revaler Str. 99, 10245 Berlin&components=country:DE|locality:Berlin&language=de&region=de&key={{google-maps-api-key}}
Accept: application/json

> {%
    client.test("the key works", function () {
        client.assert(response.body.status === "OK",
            response.body.status + " " + (response.body.error_message || ""));
    });

    // Guarded on the result existing. Without it a denied key fails all three tests with a
    // TypeError, and the one that says why scrolls away behind two that cannot say anything.
    if (response.body.status === "OK") {
        client.test("the result is inside Berlin", function () {
            const p = response.body.results[0].geometry.location;
            client.assert(p.lat > 52.30 && p.lat < 52.70, "latitude outside Berlin: " + p.lat);
            client.assert(p.lng > 13.05 && p.lng < 13.80, "longitude outside Berlin: " + p.lng);
        });
        client.test("the match is precise enough to store", function () {
            const t = response.body.results[0].geometry.location_type;
            client.assert(t === "ROOFTOP" || t === "RANGE_INTERPOLATED", "location_type is " + t);
        });
    }
%}

### v4 — an unstructured string, which may be a venue name rather than an address
### The path form. **This is the query that settled three venues #329 could not.** Asking
### for `Club AMT Berlin` comes back as a `night_club` point of interest, which answers a
### different question than asking for its address — and #329 found exactly this with
### OpenStreetMap, where a name pass caught three venues the address pass had excused.
###
### Non-ASCII is the trap. The client percent-encodes a query string but sends a path
### segment's umlaut or `ß` raw, so write those by hand as `%C3%9F` — or use the structured
### request below, where the query string does the encoding for you.
###
### The key goes in a header here, not in the URL. ijhttp logs the request line it sends, so
### a `key=` query parameter ends up in the run log; a header does not.
GET https://geocode.googleapis.com/v4/geocode/address/Club AMT Berlin?languageCode=de
X-Goog-Api-Key: {{google-maps-api-key}}
Accept: application/json

> {%
    client.test("status is 200", function () {
        client.assert(response.status === 200, "Expected 200 but got " + response.status);
    });

    // v4 answers in different words than v3: `location.latitude`, `granularity` where v3 says
    // `location_type`, and no top-level `status` at all — a bad request is an HTTP 400 carrying
    // an `error` object, so the status check above is the one that matters.
    if (response.status === 200 && response.body.results && response.body.results.length) {
        const top = response.body.results[0];
        client.log(top.location.latitude.toFixed(6) + ", " + top.location.longitude.toFixed(6));
        client.log(top.granularity + "  " + JSON.stringify(top.types));
        client.log(top.formattedAddress);
        client.test("a name lookup returns the venue, not just its street", function () {
            client.assert((top.types || []).indexOf("point_of_interest") >= 0,
                "types are " + JSON.stringify(top.types));
        });
    }
%}

### v4 — the same address as structured components
### The query-string form. Every component is prefixed `address.`, which is the one part that
### is not guessable: `addressLines`, `locality` and `postalCode` on their own are rejected as
### unknown fields, and so is a `postalAddress.` or `structuredAddress.` prefix.
###
### Prefer this whenever the street is already in separate fields, as it is on `VenueRequest`.
### It encodes umlauts for you and it never has to guess where the house number ends.
GET https://geocode.googleapis.com/v4/geocode/address?address.addressLines=Oranienstraße 190&address.locality=Berlin&address.postalCode=10999&address.regionCode=DE&languageCode=de
X-Goog-Api-Key: {{google-maps-api-key}}
Accept: application/json

> {%
    client.test("status is 200", function () {
        client.assert(response.status === 200, "Expected 200 but got " + response.status);
    });
    client.test("the structured components resolve to a building", function () {
        const top = response.body.results[0];
        client.assert(top.granularity === "ROOFTOP" || top.granularity === "RANGE_INTERPOLATED",
            "granularity is " + top.granularity);
    });
    if (response.status === 200 && response.body.results && response.body.results.length) {
        client.log(response.body.results[0].formattedAddress);
    }
%}
